  "blocks": [
    {
      "block_id": 0,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "When a data register is used as either a source or a destination operand, only the \nappropriate low-order portion is changed; the remaining high-order portion is neither used \nnor changed. \n",
      "bbox": [
        72.32999420166016,
        51.84003448486328,
        457.4677734375,
        87.5904312133789
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.1435118932572622,
        0.0798817072387358,
        0.9076741536458334,
        0.1349704577288576
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 1,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "2.3.2 Address Registers \n",
      "bbox": [
        72.5,
        98.14502716064453,
        208.36819458007812,
        113.80862426757812
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.14384920634920634,
        0.15123431927641892,
        0.413428957500155,
        0.17537077849823762
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 2,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "Each address register (and the stack pointer) is 32 bits wide and holds a full, 32-bit \naddress. Address registers do not support byte-sized operands. Therefore, when an \naddress register is used as a source operand, either the low-order word or the entire long-\nI, \nword operand is used, depending upon the operation size. When an address register is \nused as the destination operand, the entire register is affected, regardless of the operation \nsize. If the operation size is word, operands are sign-extended to 32 bits before the \noperation is performed. \n",
      "bbox": [
        72.5999984741211,
        116.63001251220703,
        497.45068359375,
        197.7504425048828
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.14404761602008154,
        0.1797183317358819,
        0.9870053245907738,
        0.30471899009091036
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 3,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "2.4 \nDATA ORGANIZATION IN MEMORY \n",
      "bbox": [
        72.97999572753906,
        211.905029296875,
        297.51873779296875,
        227.56863403320312
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.14480157882448227,
        0.32653017462114725,
        0.5903149559384301,
        0.35066664559930577
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 4,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "Bytes are individually addressable. As shown in Figure 2-5, the high-order byte of a word \nhas the same address as the word. The low-order byte has an odd address, one count \nhigher. Instructions and multibyte data are accessed only on word (even byte) boundaries. \nIf a long-word operand is located at address n (n even), then the second word of that \noperand is located at address n+2. \n",
      "bbox": [
        72.89999389648438,
        230.4000244140625,
        458.00152587890625,
        288.4704284667969
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
